WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.800
Bubble Bobble!

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:06.540
These are two hungry dino mites and they've got bubble fighting fun down tight.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:29.180
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:31.340
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:32.500
And I'm Joe.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:35.480
Guys, they're not really dinosaurs though, right?

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:37.160
They kind of look dinosaur-like.

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:39.760
I would say they are. Yeah, they've got the spines on their back and everything.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:41.520
Aren't they described as dragons?

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:42.820
Are dragons dinosaurs?

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:44.840
They're, you know, related.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.460
They're both made up, so it doesn't really matter.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.260
Nobody says potato though.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:51.640
No, it's not that they're made up.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:53.640
It's that they existed alongside humans.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:55.780
Both of them. Yeah, yeah.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:57.420
Okay, that's a good explanation too.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:01.640
And, you know, Joe, we have to get this out of the way just to get it out of your system.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:05.040
Bubble Bobble, Double Dribble, Double Dragon.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:06.480
Double Dragon, F-Zero.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.500
Would you like to enlighten anybody?

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.780
I said it in the last episode actually to get it out of my system, but...

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:13.260
Well, I'll start it up again.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:14.380
Would I like to tell everyone? Yeah.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:19.020
Yeah, there's a great YouTube video by Nirvana the Band the Show.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:25.780
I don't want to spoil it for you, but if you want to get some good Bubble Bobble, Double Dribble, Double Dragon, F-Zero action, check it out.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.260
It is the best part of...

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:58.240
It's a song.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:10.560
I'll spoil that much. You can play on a single contained screen.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:15.380
You can play on a single one of the other levels that you put them in.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.240
And you burst them by kind of like colliding with them.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:22.720
Each level has a certain number of enemies that must be defeated in order to advance.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.140
What do you guys think? I think I'm really proud of that one.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:27.420
Yeah, that pretty much sums it up.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:29.440
It's a really good one. It's a very easy to describe game.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:39.920
Yeah. But it's weird though, because when I saw the promotional material for this stuff, I was expecting a very different game.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:48.740
Because when I was a kid, I had Bust-A-Move and I thought that this was going to be like Bust-A-Move.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:51.200
Because they have the same characters in them.

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:55.780
Those dinosaur things are not what I thought they were going to do.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:01.040
Yeah, it's actually funny enough, Sean, because we usually do this in sequels and spinoffs.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:08.100
But it's the exact same thing because it's actually a spinoff in Japan called Puzzle Bobble.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.020
And they just renamed it for North America?

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:16.100
They just renamed it in North America to Bust-A-Move, but I think Puzzle Bobble is really clever on Bubble Bobble.

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:18.320
You need the context.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:19.600
Puzzle Bobble was the...

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:20.820
I'm confused. What?

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.780
Puzzle Bobble is the spinoff of this game or this game is a spinoff of...

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:31.140
No, Puzzle Bobble is the spinoff of Bubble Bobble much later on.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:38.740
But it's just weird that only Japan got that neat pun added to it and we just get a whole new game called Bust-A-Move.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:40.640
Yeah, it was very strange.

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:44.000
Maybe they thought we were like, we Americans don't like puzzles.

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:46.420
Yeah, maybe they thought that out.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:47.700
But we love dancing.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:56.100
Yeah. Okay, and Sean, just for the record, do you want to describe Bust-A-Move to people so they know why it's not like Bubble Bobble?

00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:03.780
Yeah, so Bust-A-Move was a puzzle game in which you had to...

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:18.540
It was like a match three, but the hook for this one was that you had to launch a random sequence of colored gems at gems that are sort of coming down from the sky.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:22.260
But you had to know angles and geometry.

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:24.060
It was almost like pool in a way.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:27.780
And I thought it was fun.

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:30.060
I had a lot of fun with that game back in the day.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:32.440
Yeah, I mean, it's to the point now where it's not even like a spinoff.

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:34.120
It's just its own successful franchise.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.100
I think they're still doing Bust-A-Move games.

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:40.620
Yeah, I just thought it was a weirdly named first version of that.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:42.420
So I was very surprised.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:53.140
I mean, Bubble Bobble's just confusing in general, though, because this is another classic case of a conflict of stories between the Japanese game and the North American game.

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:59.900
I don't know if you guys bothered to read the manual for Bubble Bobble, but they had a story in there that was more like a comic.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:04.140
And it's not at all what I've read is the actual story of Bubble Bobble.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:06.040
Please enlighten me.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:20.520
So the story that I've read is that, you know, a villain named Baron Von Blubba has kidnapped the brothers Bubby and Bobby's girlfriends and then turned the brothers into the Bubble Dragons Bub and Bob.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:26.460
Now Bub and Bob have to finish 100 levels in the Cave of Monsters in order to rescue them.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:30.260
I'll say that that's not my Bubble Bobble.

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:35.460
Right. Your Bubble Bobble is just two dragons going on an adventure through a fun cave, right?

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:37.380
Yeah. And they like to rhyme.

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:46.060
They're actually pretty OK with everything that's going on as long as everything stays lighthearted, you know?

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:51.940
Yeah. There's a very lighthearted message at the start of the game, too, that's just like, let's have a good story.

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:53.700
And, you know, everybody have fun.

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:54.800
It was very cheerful.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:56.520
And I just thought, like, who says that?

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:57.980
Like, let's have a good story.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:01.700
Like, you're in control of the story.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:03.600
You're the one who's telling it.

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:05.000
Yeah. Maybe it's like they're about to go.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:06.420
Now it's the beginning of a fantastic story.

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:07.900
I wish every book started like that.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:09.260
All right, everybody, let's have a good book.

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:13.740
It's like a coach before the game.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:18.760
Yeah. Let's all make sure we don't make any spelling mistakes and have a good book.

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:22.400
Yeah. That's exactly what I was getting at.

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:27.920
But, you know, before we get any further into the game, can we talk about the character designs of Bub and Bob?

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:29.180
I really like them.

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:31.840
There's something about these guys that has, like, a mascot quality to them.

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:33.440
Very mascot-y.

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:36.220
I would say they have a sensuousness to them.

00:06:36.980 --> 00:06:38.000
A sensuousness? Okay.

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:40.900
No, it's nice. They're simple.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:42.940
They're simple, but they're still distinct.

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:44.920
I think that's what makes them a standout to me.

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:52.760
Absolutely. It's like, and also, like, just the fact that you can differentiate, like, between Bub and Bob, even though they're pretty much just color palette things.

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:58.680
It reminds me of, like, the original Mario and Luigi, where the only thing that made Luigi different was that it was green instead of red.

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:02.100
Yeah. Like, it was just green Mario, and that was, like, how that joke started.

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:04.260
But it's the same thing with Bub and Bob.

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:15.660
I would say the green guy, at least in the illustrations in which there's a little bit more detail, the green guy looks a little bit more worried about what's happening.

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:18.900
And the blue guy just looks blank.

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:25.160
But that's if they ever decide to illustrate their eyebrows, which it seems to be pretty rare.

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:33.520
Well, it is also funny, because in the manual, it differentiates, oh, this one's Bub and this one's Bob, but it only does it in the black and white portion of the manual.

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:36.240
So I still don't know which one's Bub and which one's Bob.

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:38.660
I assume Bub is just the player one, but...

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:44.540
I wonder if that's just a result of your, like, because you're reading the manual online, correct?

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:50.120
Yeah. Yeah, so I wonder, like, if you paid for, like, an actual version of the manual, if it ever came in color.

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:51.920
Because that's just, like, so strange.

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:53.240
The manual cover's in color.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:56.300
I mean, what's printed in the manual is not in color.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:01.380
I mean, I think it's just literally a scan of the, you know, the Nintendo seal of approval is in color.

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:03.020
Ah. Okay, yeah.

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:04.020
I mean, look, I believe you.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:09.400
You know, I believe that there's not, like, a separate version of this that's just the black and white edition.

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:11.600
That's sort of what you were trying to tell us.

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:12.840
Yeah, I know, I know.

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:14.580
Yeah, I don't think you do believe me.

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:15.980
Let's move on from this.

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:20.080
Let's get away from character design, because that's kind of, like, out of our element anyway.

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:21.280
We're not illustrators.

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:23.740
We don't know really how to critique that kind of thing.

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:26.900
And let's get into what we do know how to critique, and that's video games.

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:30.180
So let's talk about just, like, the core gameplay loop here.

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:34.540
Is it satisfying to blow these bubbles and bobble those enemies?

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:41.240
Well, I think, I mean, as someone who has said a lot of times that, like, arcade games usually just don't do it for me.

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:46.740
This one, it just, I don't know, something about it, maybe because of all the arcade games we've played so far,

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:49.720
have given me, like, an expectation.

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:55.400
But, like, the controls, first of all, feel pretty good, compared to some other stuff.

00:08:55.540 --> 00:09:01.500
I mean, like, it's, like, definitely still a little floatier than, like, a Mario or something, like a Super Mario Brothers, rather.

00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:10.260
But otherwise, like, it doesn't feel as janky as a lot of other, like, arcade games like this that we've played.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:18.000
So that's the first thing that stood out to me, because by looking at this, I thought, okay, this is going to be, like, an Ice Climbers style, like, level of frustration.

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:19.260
But totally is not.

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:31.440
Yeah, I would say that this game also gives me similar feelings while I'm playing it as something like Joust or even Balloon Fight.

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:41.040
All the single screen games, like, the physics-based single screen games all sort of give me a similar feeling,

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:50.220
because this is, I would say that this game still has a little bit of a physics element to it with, like, the way the bubbles move around and your interactions with them.

00:09:50.500 --> 00:10:04.700
So it has that, like, yes, they're static environments, but you can still express yourself while you're playing with them, just by how you deal with your bubbles, you know?

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:10.860
Yeah, I'd like to talk about just the bubbles, too, there for a minute, because there's, like, a bunch of different types of things,

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:18.640
going on on any particular stage, even very early on in the game, where there's the bubbles you blow, which seem like, it's probably a limited amount,

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:21.240
but it's a lot of bubbles, given, you know, the state of the game.

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:27.420
And then there's bubbles that just kind of pop up that have, like, they're, like, special bubbles that do different things for you.

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:35.120
And then there's all the different enemies that you also, like, when you capture them into bubbles, you don't have to, like, burst them right away,

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:37.000
and they're kind of floating around on the screen, too.

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:44.940
So there's this kind of, even though it's on a single screen, it invites a lot of chaos into the game in a meaningful way, though, you know?

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:47.140
It's sort of like your bubble bubble juggling.

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:49.220
Yeah, okay. Juggle juggle.

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:52.040
Sure, yeah, that's what they were actually thinking about calling the game.

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:53.100
I know about that.

00:10:53.660 --> 00:10:56.060
Juggle juggle. Here's the thing, though, Sean.

00:10:56.440 --> 00:11:03.320
So, because you brought up the juggling thing, did you ever at any point feel overwhelmed by all this stuff on the screen?

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:11.160
Because that's usually something we talk about, that, like, in Shmups, there's just eventually a point later on where they just add too many enemies to the screen, and you're more likely to die.

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:22.520
I wouldn't say it was a feeling of being overwhelmed, but it was definitely over time, over the amount of levels that I played, more things started to happen.

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:26.040
But it wasn't, like, an anxiety-inducing thing.

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:28.020
It was just like, oh, this is just more stuff.

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:32.160
And I don't know what the difference is there, but I felt it.

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:41.380
There does become, like, quite a saturation of things on the screen, but the thing about it is that most of the new things that are appearing on the screen are usually helpful things.

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:45.980
It's not like enemies just keep spawning and overwhelming you in that way.

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:50.160
So maybe you might get overwhelmed with, like, choices of, like, okay, do I jump on the bubbles that are already here?

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:51.840
Do I make a bubble to jump on?

00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:53.440
And stuff like that.

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:57.880
But otherwise, I don't think I was overwhelmed with, like, oh, my God, there's too many enemies around.

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:00.420
That's a good way to describe it, I'd say, yeah.

00:12:01.060 --> 00:12:06.740
Yeah, and there's, like, a limit to the amount of rules in this game that, like, are established, you know?

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:09.940
And that never, like, changes or becomes more dynamic.

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:18.580
It's just that the, you know, the enemy variety increases or the way that the platform is, like, sorry, the way that the stage is platformed out.

00:12:18.580 --> 00:12:21.840
It's usually into, like, cute, like, shapes of a heart or shapes of a smiley face.

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:25.060
You know, like, those are the things that change every stage to make them fun.

00:12:25.540 --> 00:12:30.820
But the actual, like, rules of the game is just blow the bubbles onto the enemy and then burst them.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:35.800
So it's, like, there's not a lot going against you.

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:40.060
You know, it's not as overwhelming as, say, in a shmup where you have to be, like, checking in every single direction

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:44.360
and watching out for this enemy who moves a different way or shoots a different type of thing.

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:46.560
Or there's a hazard on the stage.

00:12:46.580 --> 00:12:48.660
You know, there's no hazards other than the enemy.

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:53.240
Yeah, and you mentioned that, like, the different, like, layouts of the levels.

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:57.520
And I think that that, you know, in addition to your description of the game,

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:02.120
those, like, the different layouts of the levels are really important to understanding the game.

00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:05.360
Because for me, that's what kind of makes this game.

00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:11.020
I mean, I think that not every level, but a lot of the levels are actually pretty cleverly designed in a way

00:13:11.020 --> 00:13:14.500
where you have to think about it very differently than you might have had to think about the last level.

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:17.140
Granted, there are a lot of misses where it's just like,

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:19.400
oh, okay, they just made, like, a funny design in the middle of this one.

00:13:19.580 --> 00:13:23.800
But there are some where, you know, the physics of the platforms there, you know,

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:26.580
where, like, you have to, you can only go through them when you're going up

00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:28.560
and you get stuck in them when you can't go down.

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:29.700
You have to, like, go back around.

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:32.540
It makes you have to think, I'm doing a poor job of explaining that,

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:35.500
but it makes you have to think about, like, how to approach enemies.

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:37.020
Yeah. Where enemies can get stuck.

00:13:37.180 --> 00:13:39.260
You don't want to get stuck in a small space with an enemy,

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:41.800
but you can go around.

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:45.080
You can loop around the top of the screen and back to the bottom

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:47.160
to get to places that you might not have been able to access.

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:52.400
There's just, the level design, like I said, some were kind of lackluster,

00:13:52.580 --> 00:13:53.960
but a lot of them were pretty cool.

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:55.660
And it's frequently changing, too.

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:58.500
No particular stage takes all that long.

00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:03.400
So you're constantly feeling this progression of, like, level one, level two, level three.

00:14:03.400 --> 00:14:06.500
You know, it's like, you can get to level 18 in this game pretty fast

00:14:06.500 --> 00:14:09.380
in a first-time playthrough, you know?

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:13.740
Yeah. Particularly because there's no real consequence to dying.

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:15.660
You can start back at the level that you died on.

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:17.480
But I also just meant the pace of the game, Joe.

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:23.140
You know, like, how it's not like each level is a five-minute-long adventure.

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:27.320
Right. You know? Like, I kind of like that they kind of come in 60-second or less bursts.

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:29.980
Yeah, and they're all sort of, like, just their own.

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:33.980
Like, it's definitely also a puzzle platformer because, well,

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:39.840
so a lot of them are just, like, fun little, like, pixel art backgrounds

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:40.960
that you have to navigate.

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:45.340
Some of them are definitely designed to be approached in a certain way,

00:14:45.340 --> 00:14:49.540
and there are little, like, traps sprung that you can fall into

00:14:49.540 --> 00:14:55.860
and also funnels that the enemies can, you can very quickly dispatch your enemies.

00:14:56.680 --> 00:15:01.540
So, I mean, it's, like, it's hit and miss, but they do get creative

00:15:01.540 --> 00:15:02.820
with the level design in there.

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:07.920
Yeah, there's a level early on in the game, maybe in, like, the teens,

00:15:08.800 --> 00:15:12.720
where that idea of, like, puzzle platforming really comes out to play because,

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:14.780
and I think this is the one you're talking about, Sean, with the funnel,

00:15:15.180 --> 00:15:18.600
where you can't get in there, and all the enemies are in there,

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:22.760
and you have to figure out how to use the power-up bubbles,

00:15:22.760 --> 00:15:26.400
how to get those to, like, slide through the funnel

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:30.220
and take out the enemies that way because your bubbles won't reach them otherwise.

00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:32.840
Yeah, that's one of the ones I was talking about.

00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:34.500
I think it was heart-shaped.

00:15:34.840 --> 00:15:36.120
Yes, it was kind of heart-shaped.

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:38.420
Yeah, it might have actually been the heart-shaped level,

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:39.680
but it was definitely like a heart.

00:15:40.240 --> 00:15:43.600
I think that was a necessary step, though, for the game, too.

00:15:43.760 --> 00:15:47.580
Like, it didn't have to make the game any more complicated.

00:15:47.580 --> 00:15:52.840
Now you instead had to think about how you approach the enemies

00:15:52.840 --> 00:15:55.020
because you can't use your bubbles to just blow.

00:15:55.180 --> 00:15:58.440
Like, I like that bubbles don't transcend through platforms,

00:15:58.440 --> 00:16:03.160
or when you do blow a bubble and it goes up into the stage

00:16:03.160 --> 00:16:06.540
because they kind of just float upwards, that it doesn't catch an enemy.

00:16:06.640 --> 00:16:10.560
Like, you can't trap an enemy by getting at a bubble that you blew earlier

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:12.600
that just happens to, like, collide in with your enemy.

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:14.400
I'm glad that that detection was removed.

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:16.220
It was a part of it at some point?

00:16:16.220 --> 00:16:20.600
No, I'm saying, like, that you could argue that, like,

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:23.920
any of the bubbles that you blow on stage should be able to catch enemies,

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:28.320
but it's only in that very first instance that you blow it

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:29.860
that it's able to capture an enemy.

00:16:30.020 --> 00:16:34.840
Yeah. Once it starts rising, it cannot capture an enemy.

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:37.820
You can burst it for maybe, like, 10 points or something like that,

00:16:37.900 --> 00:16:39.320
which is kind of fun. Yeah.

00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:42.260
But, you know, it would have been a weird consequence

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:45.460
to just be able to blow bubbles freely,

00:16:45.460 --> 00:16:48.020
and if any enemies happen to bump into those bubbles,

00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:49.620
they are now captured.

00:16:50.260 --> 00:16:58.320
I would say, though, that if there was some tweaks to other elements of the game

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:05.340
and you, like, that skill ceiling of, like, oh, can you get this guy with just a rising bubble,

00:17:05.340 --> 00:17:07.380
I think that would be interesting as well.

00:17:07.600 --> 00:17:08.860
But you're right.

00:17:08.860 --> 00:17:13.180
The way that the game is at this very moment, that would have been a little goofy.

00:17:13.340 --> 00:17:15.720
Yeah, I think that it's also kind of cool that, like, you know,

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:18.600
you shoot the bubble, and while it's on its forward path,

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:20.340
on its immediate forward path,

00:17:20.460 --> 00:17:23.340
if it's a direct hit, it'll capture the enemy.

00:17:23.340 --> 00:17:26.240
But otherwise, it exists, it can't capture the enemy,

00:17:26.320 --> 00:17:29.680
but you can still use it to your advantage to help you get to higher places,

00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:32.280
you can jump off of it, you can ride it to the top of the level.

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:34.780
And I thought that was cool that it's, like, you have,

00:17:34.920 --> 00:17:37.900
it's the same move, but you have multiple uses for it.

00:17:37.940 --> 00:17:40.100
And I don't know how you guys felt about this, but,

00:17:40.300 --> 00:17:44.640
and maybe I wasn't following exactly with how the game wanted me to understand

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:46.620
the platforming of the levels,

00:17:46.800 --> 00:17:50.160
but later levels that have a lot more platforming in them,

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:56.720
there's kind of, like, some weird unspoken rules to what you can collide into,

00:17:56.760 --> 00:17:59.940
and what you can pass over, and what you can land on,

00:18:01.960 --> 00:18:05.260
that just isn't that well communicated to the player,

00:18:05.300 --> 00:18:09.220
and is distracting in a way, because you kind of think, like,

00:18:09.400 --> 00:18:11.260
oh, I can get over this, this, this, and this,

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:14.300
but then it turns out it's actually all just solid.

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:21.500
It's basically, you can only pass through a platform from below,

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:26.040
and even if you're trying to jump to another platform,

00:18:26.300 --> 00:18:31.300
if you accidentally clip one between you and the other platform,

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:32.640
you will land on that one.

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:35.420
It is a little annoying, but I guess you can,

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:39.760
if you get better at figuring out the arc of your jump,

00:18:39.760 --> 00:18:44.740
you can get rid of that, but yeah, it's this weird feeling like,

00:18:44.860 --> 00:18:47.180
oh, I can jump through this, but I can't throw a bubble through it,

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:53.260
and it's sort of like this weird, like, feeling one thing and seeing another,

00:18:54.260 --> 00:18:56.060
but no, I get what you're saying there.

00:18:56.540 --> 00:18:59.880
Yeah, I was actually a little disappointed at one point,

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:01.120
because when I first started playing it,

00:19:01.180 --> 00:19:03.520
I was playing it with a friend of the podcast, Mark,

00:19:03.640 --> 00:19:05.320
who I've mentioned before, and a couple other friends,

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:08.400
and we at first thought, like,

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:13.960
because there are different textures of the platforms you're on,

00:19:14.140 --> 00:19:16.360
I started to notice, like, oh, like, when it's this texture,

00:19:16.500 --> 00:19:18.920
you can jump through it from the bottom and land on it from the top,

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:22.040
and we'll learn that, like, each texture has a different type of physics,

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:24.200
and then later they'll start introducing new, like,

00:19:24.300 --> 00:19:25.920
old textures in with other textures,

00:19:25.980 --> 00:19:28.340
and you just remember what each one does.

00:19:28.540 --> 00:19:29.600
It never really does that.

00:19:29.960 --> 00:19:32.460
Like, it's just all new textures.

00:19:32.900 --> 00:19:35.220
I thought you had learned a secret of the game.

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:38.280
No, it seemed like it was going that way,

00:19:38.360 --> 00:19:39.440
and then it ends up just being like,

00:19:39.540 --> 00:19:40.600
no, they're just all these different textures,

00:19:40.660 --> 00:19:43.380
and they seem to do the same thing,

00:19:43.460 --> 00:19:46.100
but sometimes I feel like they did different things,

00:19:46.180 --> 00:19:48.840
and it was never clear, like, can you only jump?

00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:52.540
I think in the end you can only jump through things from the bottom,

00:19:52.700 --> 00:19:54.640
but there were some times it felt like that didn't work.

00:19:55.440 --> 00:19:59.480
Maybe that was just because of little, like, weird glitchy things in the game,

00:19:59.540 --> 00:20:03.340
but I just was excited with the idea of, like,

00:20:03.340 --> 00:20:06.260
oh, there's these, each of these different textures behave a different way,

00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:07.420
and I'll have to learn what they are,

00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:09.160
and it's just kind of not true.

00:20:09.400 --> 00:20:10.480
It might even have to do with, like,

00:20:10.500 --> 00:20:12.780
how close the platforms are to other platforms,

00:20:12.820 --> 00:20:15.440
like collision detection that just pushes you back out

00:20:15.440 --> 00:20:16.740
or doesn't let you get through at all.

00:20:16.820 --> 00:20:18.000
I think that's correct as well.

00:20:24.020 --> 00:20:25.840
So, I don't know about you guys,

00:20:26.300 --> 00:20:29.200
enemy designs in NES games,

00:20:29.400 --> 00:20:31.360
they're hit or miss for most of the time,

00:20:31.480 --> 00:20:33.200
but I always think about Stinger,

00:20:33.560 --> 00:20:35.800
a game that is currently on our Essential Games list,

00:20:36.060 --> 00:20:37.740
as, like, peak enemy design

00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:39.700
because it was just so wacky and goofy

00:20:39.700 --> 00:20:42.420
and, like, every single level just introduced,

00:20:42.540 --> 00:20:44.840
like, a whole new set of enemies each time

00:20:44.840 --> 00:20:45.980
to the point where you're fighting, like,

00:20:46.240 --> 00:20:48.260
microwaves and televisions at a certain point.

00:20:48.460 --> 00:20:51.400
I also do love to fight saxophones, for sure.

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:53.380
Oh, yeah, it was a giant saxophone, right?

00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:55.620
Yeah. That was an enemy, a boss battle, actually.

00:20:57.000 --> 00:20:58.100
In Bubble Bobble,

00:20:58.280 --> 00:21:00.260
even though these aren't necessarily, like,

00:21:00.260 --> 00:21:02.040
as creative as a microwave or a television,

00:21:02.300 --> 00:21:04.660
they had a Stinger-like quality to them,

00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:06.760
and I thought all the enemies were really, like,

00:21:06.840 --> 00:21:09.560
fun and playful and not, like...

00:21:10.220 --> 00:21:12.200
Yeah, there was nothing that was, like, in my way.

00:21:12.200 --> 00:21:14.660
I think I could even argue that some of these enemy designs

00:21:14.660 --> 00:21:16.720
are more creative than a microwave or a television.

00:21:17.640 --> 00:21:20.920
I think that since they're all distinct

00:21:20.920 --> 00:21:23.100
and they're not ugly

00:21:23.100 --> 00:21:26.200
and you're never really wondering what they are,

00:21:26.480 --> 00:21:27.480
I think it works.

00:21:27.780 --> 00:21:31.460
And, like, for 8-bit design,

00:21:31.460 --> 00:21:34.800
I think that's one of the biggest boxes you have to check

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:38.340
is just as long as you can distinguish between them.

00:21:38.500 --> 00:21:41.560
And they do introduce a lot of new ones, like, as you go.

00:21:41.560 --> 00:21:42.840
Even, like, later in...

00:21:42.840 --> 00:21:43.640
I mean, I didn't...

00:21:43.640 --> 00:21:45.420
I guess there's a lot of levels in this game.

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:47.900
I got to, like, somewhere in the 40s or 50s.

00:21:48.380 --> 00:21:50.700
But even then, there's, like... 100 levels. Is it only 100?

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:52.300
I thought there was, like, 140,

00:21:52.620 --> 00:21:54.320
and then I thought there were two versions.

00:21:54.500 --> 00:21:56.320
There's 200. There's 200?

00:21:57.120 --> 00:21:59.440
It says that there's game one

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:02.920
and, like, there's 100 and some levels in that,

00:22:02.940 --> 00:22:04.440
and then there's game two

00:22:04.440 --> 00:22:06.960
that you can only play after you beat game one.

00:22:07.300 --> 00:22:08.660
Oh, okay. There's a man in there somewhere.

00:22:08.880 --> 00:22:10.280
I know what you're talking about.

00:22:10.440 --> 00:22:12.280
Yeah, yeah, yeah. I didn't know that those were...

00:22:12.280 --> 00:22:14.300
I thought you have to play through the game

00:22:14.300 --> 00:22:15.460
and, like, a harder thing,

00:22:15.620 --> 00:22:17.580
but it turned... Because I didn't beat the game, obviously.

00:22:18.020 --> 00:22:18.880
So I see what you're saying.

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:20.440
After you beat the first 100 levels

00:22:20.440 --> 00:22:22.540
and you have to go play, like, the secret mode,

00:22:22.700 --> 00:22:24.740
that's a whole other 100 set of levels.

00:22:25.520 --> 00:22:26.700
I believe so. Yeah, which is crazy.

00:22:27.260 --> 00:22:29.020
But my point being that, like,

00:22:29.040 --> 00:22:30.700
even in, like, that far into the game,

00:22:30.840 --> 00:22:32.080
they're introducing new enemies

00:22:32.080 --> 00:22:33.420
that look distinct and look...

00:22:33.420 --> 00:22:35.660
You know, at one point, there's, like, something that looks to me

00:22:35.660 --> 00:22:37.540
almost exactly like a proto-pet

00:22:37.540 --> 00:22:38.540
from Ratchet & Clank,

00:22:38.580 --> 00:22:40.040
if anyone is a Ratchet & Clank fan.

00:22:40.400 --> 00:22:41.720
But, you know, it just, like,

00:22:41.760 --> 00:22:44.040
it has that level of, like, character to it.

00:22:44.100 --> 00:22:46.540
And each thing has its own distinct character

00:22:46.540 --> 00:22:47.360
design to it.

00:22:47.480 --> 00:22:49.620
Yeah, and a lot of these also look like

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:51.540
they belong in a Mega Man game, too.

00:22:51.820 --> 00:22:53.680
You know? Yeah, they have that style to it.

00:22:53.780 --> 00:22:56.260
Yeah. So I think, you know,

00:22:56.360 --> 00:22:57.960
we're definitely hitting, like,

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:00.160
the right amount of...

00:23:00.160 --> 00:23:01.220
For an arcade game, too.

00:23:01.340 --> 00:23:02.960
Like, the graphics are nothing...

00:23:02.960 --> 00:23:03.960
They're nothing impressive,

00:23:03.960 --> 00:23:05.760
but they're also simple enough

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:06.940
that it just works.

00:23:06.940 --> 00:23:07.920
It wasn't distracting.

00:23:07.940 --> 00:23:10.280
It's not, like, poor today.

00:23:10.820 --> 00:23:14.900
It's still, like, a fun visual game to play, too.

00:23:15.060 --> 00:23:16.100
Right. I think it shows that, like,

00:23:16.100 --> 00:23:17.680
it's not necessarily about how good

00:23:17.680 --> 00:23:19.940
the actual graphical fidelity is.

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:22.360
It's just, like, the design is that good.

00:23:22.600 --> 00:23:23.740
Like, using those graphics,

00:23:23.860 --> 00:23:24.760
it's just designed well.

00:23:25.140 --> 00:23:26.740
Yeah. So we were talking about

00:23:27.540 --> 00:23:28.500
the platforming

00:23:28.500 --> 00:23:31.800
and how that was kind of confusing at times.

00:23:31.800 --> 00:23:33.000
And the only reason why

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:34.500
I really brought that out at the start anyway,

00:23:34.520 --> 00:23:36.180
because I'm sure that there is

00:23:36.180 --> 00:23:37.220
an established rule

00:23:37.220 --> 00:23:38.380
to how the platform works,

00:23:38.440 --> 00:23:40.900
but I was bringing it up more because if the pace of the game

00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:43.480
is part of the enjoyment of everything,

00:23:44.360 --> 00:23:46.540
nothing... nothing stinks more

00:23:46.540 --> 00:23:47.520
than when you're kind of, like,

00:23:47.700 --> 00:23:48.680
stuck on one thing

00:23:48.680 --> 00:23:50.260
and you're waiting for an enemy

00:23:50.260 --> 00:23:51.480
to come to you

00:23:51.480 --> 00:23:53.500
or to come around to an area

00:23:53.500 --> 00:23:55.760
that you can access, and then you just have to hear

00:23:55.760 --> 00:23:57.560
that one music track

00:23:58.000 --> 00:23:59.420
over and over and over again

00:23:59.420 --> 00:24:01.160
while you wait. And whether you're waiting

00:24:01.160 --> 00:24:03.120
or you're playing, it's only one music track.

00:24:03.400 --> 00:24:07.200
Yeah. It's sort of turned into wallpaper for me.

00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:10.160
It wasn't annoying enough

00:24:10.160 --> 00:24:14.240
for me to get that upset with it

00:24:14.800 --> 00:24:16.580
like many other games do,

00:24:16.740 --> 00:24:18.660
but it's definitely a con.

00:24:18.840 --> 00:24:20.660
Yeah, I do wonder how long the loop was.

00:24:20.760 --> 00:24:22.000
Was it 10 seconds?

00:24:22.140 --> 00:24:22.980
Was it 30 seconds?

00:24:23.180 --> 00:24:25.540
I couldn't focus long enough to count,

00:24:25.740 --> 00:24:27.640
but I do wonder

00:24:27.640 --> 00:24:29.200
because you would think

00:24:29.200 --> 00:24:31.980
that maybe every 10 levels

00:24:31.980 --> 00:24:34.520
you could change up the music a bit

00:24:34.520 --> 00:24:36.280
or just the pitch or something,

00:24:36.300 --> 00:24:38.120
but we got what we got,

00:24:38.180 --> 00:24:40.220
and I think if music's the only complaint,

00:24:40.500 --> 00:24:41.440
I mean, you know,

00:24:41.560 --> 00:24:43.920
we already have a lot of great soundtracks

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:45.800
on the NES. I'll forgive Bubble Bobble

00:24:45.800 --> 00:24:47.620
for not including more than one song,

00:24:47.700 --> 00:24:49.380
I suppose. Yeah.

00:24:50.180 --> 00:24:51.400
This game isn't that bad

00:24:51.400 --> 00:24:52.580
because there are better games.

00:24:56.440 --> 00:24:58.580
And Joe, you mentioned this way early on,

00:24:58.720 --> 00:24:59.920
but you can continue

00:24:59.920 --> 00:25:01.220
right where you left off

00:25:01.220 --> 00:25:02.840
as many times as you like,

00:25:02.920 --> 00:25:03.960
so there's really no reason

00:25:03.960 --> 00:25:04.940
not to beat this game

00:25:04.940 --> 00:25:05.800
for any of us. We just,

00:25:05.900 --> 00:25:07.000
I guess it couldn't hold our attention

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:08.320
because you can just continue

00:25:08.800 --> 00:25:10.100
until your heart's content.

00:25:10.560 --> 00:25:12.280
You just, you have three lives,

00:25:12.300 --> 00:25:13.960
I think, when you start each game,

00:25:14.040 --> 00:25:14.960
but as soon as you die,

00:25:15.340 --> 00:25:16.480
they give you a password,

00:25:16.560 --> 00:25:18.580
but as long as you haven't reset your cartridge,

00:25:18.860 --> 00:25:20.140
you can just go right back

00:25:20.140 --> 00:25:21.800
into the level, no password needed.

00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:26.020
Oh, yeah. I guess it's just weird to me

00:25:26.020 --> 00:25:29.120
that a game that the loop,

00:25:29.540 --> 00:25:30.460
like the gameplay loop

00:25:30.460 --> 00:25:32.940
is so simple that it does have a password thing.

00:25:34.240 --> 00:25:35.240
Like, I would expect

00:25:35.240 --> 00:25:36.660
this more adventure games

00:25:36.660 --> 00:25:37.760
or action games

00:25:37.760 --> 00:25:40.020
with very distinct levels,

00:25:40.460 --> 00:25:42.280
but I guess, like, yeah, if there's an end state

00:25:42.280 --> 00:25:44.040
and it's 200 levels away,

00:25:44.260 --> 00:25:45.780
then you better have

00:25:45.780 --> 00:25:46.620
a password system.

00:25:46.860 --> 00:25:48.020
Yeah, I think it actually comes back

00:25:48.020 --> 00:25:49.020
to that let's have

00:25:49.020 --> 00:25:50.120
a good story thing.

00:25:50.260 --> 00:25:51.400
You can't promise you're going to have

00:25:51.400 --> 00:25:53.020
a good story and then make people

00:25:53.020 --> 00:25:54.520
start at, you know,

00:25:54.580 --> 00:25:56.520
the very beginning every single time

00:25:56.520 --> 00:25:57.500
they want to play the game. Right.

00:25:57.860 --> 00:25:59.640
I mean, what is the, do you see a story?

00:26:00.340 --> 00:26:01.540
No, no, no, I'm joking.

00:26:01.540 --> 00:26:03.760
I'm saying that they said there's a story,

00:26:03.800 --> 00:26:04.820
so you could argue

00:26:04.820 --> 00:26:06.200
that this is an adventure game,

00:26:06.300 --> 00:26:07.760
Sean. That's true. You're right.

00:26:08.260 --> 00:26:10.220
But I, again,

00:26:10.300 --> 00:26:11.220
I think this might just be my,

00:26:11.380 --> 00:26:12.340
because of my personal

00:26:12.340 --> 00:26:13.340
preference against, you know,

00:26:13.420 --> 00:26:14.860
about arcade games,

00:26:14.860 --> 00:26:16.660
but for me,

00:26:16.800 --> 00:26:17.980
just since it's not

00:26:17.980 --> 00:26:18.800
my thing generally,

00:26:19.180 --> 00:26:20.300
the ability to just pick up

00:26:20.300 --> 00:26:21.460
where I left off

00:26:21.460 --> 00:26:23.280
is essential to me.

00:26:23.460 --> 00:26:24.300
Not saying this is an

00:26:24.300 --> 00:26:26.120
essential game. That's an essential ability

00:26:26.120 --> 00:26:28.660
because for an arcade game,

00:26:28.660 --> 00:26:30.100
I don't want to die

00:26:30.100 --> 00:26:31.300
and have to redo levels

00:26:31.300 --> 00:26:33.560
I already did because I think that's

00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:34.600
where I get kind of bored

00:26:34.600 --> 00:26:36.860
with the loop. So it was nice to be able

00:26:36.860 --> 00:26:38.040
to just continue to move on

00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:38.980
even after I died.

00:26:38.980 --> 00:26:39.900
I know that's not

00:26:39.900 --> 00:26:41.060
necessarily like the,

00:26:41.060 --> 00:26:42.800
um, the, uh,

00:26:43.120 --> 00:26:44.060
purest video game,

